Game of Thrones provided unforgettable  moments near-perfect episodes and  stunning seasons throughout its run but  as it continued it also changed  television in many ways including a few  more subtle changes that flew slightly  under the radar as Game of Thrones  started its run it seemed to have a  clear main character that viewers were  supposed to rally behind and that  character was Ned Stark as the  Honourable patriarch of the Highborne  Stark family the warden of the north  was lured away from his family and life  at Winterfell to head to King's Landing  the capital of Westeros to become the  hand of the king  in doing so he splinters the stark  family across the map of Westeros  leaving his wife and sons behind as he  and his daughters make a new home in  King's Landing however things take a  turn for the worse when Ned discovers  that the heirs to the throne aren't  actually the children of King Robert  Baratheon but are instead the children  of Queen Cersei and her brother in the  penultimate episode of the first season  he confesses to his crimes in front of  the entire populace of King's Landing  ultimately losing his head anyway fans  of the book saw this moment coming but  for those who had just started with the  show it remains one of the best moments  on Thrones perfectly setting up  expectations before yanking the rug out  from under the entire audience from  older films like Venn her to more recent  epics like the Lord of the Rings trilogy  movies have always been afforded the  budgets and scope to stage enormous  action sequences but that opportunity  has largely been absent from television  even prestige dramas like Breaking Bad  and Mad Men while beautifully shot  remained relatively modest in scope but  all of that changed with Game of Thrones  easily the most technically ambitious  show in television history Thrones  filming spanned continents shooting in  exotic locales like Iceland and Croatia  as it gave fans bigger and bigger  sequences there's a brave men knocking  at our door  let's go kill them while relatively low  budgets during season one prevented the  showrunners from shooting battle scenes  that all change with season two's  Blackwater which led to even more  expensive sequences down the line battle  of the bastards the climax of season six  set new standards and in the show's  final season the Battle of Winterfell  ended up becoming the longest ever  battle shot for television or film for  years television couldn't compete with  film on an epic scale Browns proved it  was not only possible but could yield  some pretty unforgettable results before  a game of Thrones it probably seemed  like a sprawling dense fantasy series  stood little chance of becoming HBO's  biggest hit of all time shortly after  the show's premier in 2011 however the  network clearly realized that they had a  massive success on their hands and got  to work making sure that the show  runners had as much money as they needed  and you will be speaking to me with more  respect  much of the added expense went to battle  sequences with the Battle of Blackwater  Bay bumping the show up to a staggering  8 million dollars for the episode even  then showrunners reportedly had to fight  almost as hard as their characters to  get that increase however by the time  the show reached its final season in  2019 it was breaking records once again  with a budget increase that brought each  episode to a whopping 15 million dollars  and some episodes like the one featuring  the Battle of Winterfell  may have even surpassed that in recent  years 10 million dollar budgets for  prestige TV had been doled out more  commonly but a budget for the final  season that reached 90 million dollars  is pretty unprecedented making it clear  that HBO would spare no expense when the  time came to finish off their signature  show with massive budgets reportedly  already set for the spin-offs it looks  like HBO has few regrets for the first  five seasons of the show Game of Thrones  stuck pretty faithfully to its source  material the novel series a Song of Ice  and Fire by George RR martin which is  currently comprised of five books named  for the first book in the series Thrones  tackled all of its biggest moments  including med Starks execution the  infamous red wedding the doomed wedding  of Joffrey Baratheon and Jon Snow's  death before moving past its source  material in season 6 season 6 came on  the heels of a particularly brutal fifth  season which contained some of the most  hard to watch scenes in the series many  critics and fans believed that once the  show could no longer follow Martin's  strict plot map even though he was a  frequent and consultant on the show the  quality suffered whether or not it was  truly able to hold its own against  Martin's books it still marks a turning  point for adaptations source material is  almost always finished by the time  adaptations are filmed so for the  showrunners to even attempt to move  beyond Martin's books while still  maintaining this story was a massive  feat shock value will always be  synonymous with Game of Thrones viewers  were traumatized by the red wedding and  horrified by Hart home but they could  also cheer as Jon and Sansa defeated  Ramsay Bolton at the Battle of the  bastards as the night king met his  defeat at the Battle of Winterfell or as  Arya changed faces to get her revenge on  Walder Frey  thousand the North remembers tell them  winter came for House Frey that being  said this shock value wasn't always the  show's most valuable or even enjoyable  quality throughout its run it pushed to  new boundaries for what viewers could  withstand the red wedding was bad enough  but watching multiple episodes featuring  graphic torture scenes or the show's  many instances of assaults as a plot  device often felt like too much even so  Browns set new standards for shock value  and if future ambitious show runners can  learn something from its triumphs and  its failures in equal measure it can  only improve the television landscape  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite stuff are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

